The library's room-booking page worked yesterday. Today the browser waits,
then gives up. The weather page still loads. Is the wireless connection down?
Is the library's name wrong? Is the booking service unavailable while the
rest of the library site is fine? These are different questions, and a good
terminal observation narrows one of them instead of answering all of them
with ``the network is broken.''

\begin{objectives}
\item Separate a host name, an address, a transport port, and a URL path.
\item Make read-only observations locally and interpret a practice report.
\item Find the most useful next question without testing someone else's system.
\end{objectives}

\section{Read the address you were given}\label{sec:37-address}
\index{host name}\index{IP address}\index{DNS}\index{port}
Suppose the library gives you
\nolinkurl{https://booking.example.org:443/rooms/tuesday}. This printed address
is a teaching example, not a real booking endpoint. The browser, or another
program making the request on your behalf, is the \FirstUse{client}. The
program answering is a \FirstUse{service}, running on a server. People often
use ``server'' for either the program or the machine that runs it. A single
machine can offer more than one service, and a service can be spread over
multiple machines.

The address is a URL, which tells the client how to request something. The
scheme \texttt{https} means to use HTTP with a protected connection. The
host name \texttt{booking.example.org} identifies the destination by name.
The explicit \texttt{:443} names the port; for HTTPS it is common to omit
the port and let the client use the default, 443. The
\texttt{/rooms/tuesday} part asks the web service for a particular resource.
It is not a directory you can assume exists on that server's disk. A program
can generate it on the spot, and a site can move it without changing its
name or port. Figure~\ref{fig:37-url} puts the pieces next to the decisions
they control.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-37/url.tikz}
\caption{Read a URL from the scheme through the resource path.}\label{fig:37-url}
\VisualDescription{Four connected labels separate HTTPS, the host name, port 443, and the rooms path.}
\end{figure}

You can parse the example without visiting it. In a shell, \cmd|printf|
with the format \cmd|'%s\n'| prints its argument as text and ends the line.
Quoting keeps the example one argument; the command does not look up the
name, open the URL, or test the site's certificate.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'https://booking.example.org:443/rooms/tuesday'
\end{terminal}

Another address might end in \texttt{?day=tuesday}. The query after the
question mark can change what the service returns without changing the
destination machine. Some real query strings include invitation codes or
temporary account tokens. Do not put real sign-in links into shell history
or support notes. Even a URL without a password can contain a private
document title or an identifier that grants access. If a support person
only needs the scheme and host, leave out the rest.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.2in}Y}
\toprule
Piece & What changing just this piece might change \\
\midrule
https & How the client talks to the service; don't switch to HTTP to bypass an error. \\
Host name & Which service name the client looks up and checks. \\
443 & Which transport endpoint the client asks for at its destination. \\
/rooms/tuesday & What resource the already contacted web service is asked to return. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Names get you to addresses, not to pages}
\FirstUse{DNS}, the Domain Name System, distributes information about names.
For a routine web connection, your system's name lookup usually consults
DNS to find candidate IP addresses, though local configuration or a cached
answer can supply an address too. The program may try more than one answer.
A common reason is that it can reach a service over IPv6 as well as IPv4,
two versions of the Internet Protocol. An IPv4 address looks like
\texttt{192.0.2.25}; an IPv6 address might look like \texttt{2001:db8::25}.
Both here are from documentation ranges. The double colon in the IPv6
example shortens a run of zero groups. In a URL containing a numeric IPv6
address, brackets separate the address from a following port, as in
\texttt{https://[2001:db8::25]:443/}. Do not substitute an address for a
name to bypass a web certificate warning: the service can rely on the name
for both identity and site selection.

A name can have many addresses for ordinary reasons. A large service might
direct readers in different regions to different addresses, and a home
computer might change addresses when it joins a different network. Likewise,
many site names can share one address. An IP address is a delivery
destination at the network layer, not proof that you reached the person or
organization you intended to reach. That distinction matters again for
SSH in Chapter~\ref{ch:38}.

Here is a made-up lookup report. It deliberately does not copy a particular
utility's format. Read each row as a candidate, not as a successful visit.

\begin{terminaloutput}
name                 family    candidate
booking.example.org  IPv4      192.0.2.25
booking.example.org  IPv6      2001:db8::25
\end{terminaloutput}

The report has no port, status code, or room availability. A correct
address answer can coexist with a down service. The reverse can also be
true: another device may reach a service immediately using a cached
answer while your device gets a lookup error. A \FirstUse{resolver} is the
component that seeks the answer. Other programs on the same machine can
use different caches or lookup paths: some browsers, for instance, can
use their own DNS settings. Don't treat your shell and browser as identical
instruments unless you know they're using the same settings.
Figure~\ref{fig:37-lookup} puts the connection attempt after address choice.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-37/lookup.tikz}
\caption{Name lookup supplies candidates for a separate connection attempt.}\label{fig:37-lookup}
\VisualDescription{The resolver hands two candidate addresses to the client, which separately attempts a service connection.}
\end{figure}

\section{Read only what you need locally}\label{sec:37-local}
Your own operating system knows about its network \FirstUse{interfaces},
which are entry and exit points for network traffic. Wi-Fi and Ethernet are
familiar kinds, and software can provide virtual ones. One kind that
doesn't lead out of your computer is \FirstUse{loopback}: the machine
talking to itself. An IPv4 loopback address you often see is
\texttt{127.0.0.1}; the IPv6 loopback address is \texttt{::1}. The usual
interface name on Linux is \texttt{lo}; on macOS and the BSDs here it is
usually \texttt{lo0}. ``This machine can see its loopback interface'' is a
small and useful fact. It does not mean your Wi-Fi, Internet connection, or
library page is up.

\index{ip@\texttt{ip}}\index{ifconfig@\texttt{ifconfig}}\index{loopback}
\begin{platformnote}{Linux and macOS/BSD}
On Linux, use the iproute2 utility \texttt{ip} if installed:
\end{platformnote}

\begin{terminal}
ip address show dev lo
\end{terminal}

In this invocation \texttt{address} selects protocol address information,
\texttt{show} asks to display it, and \texttt{dev lo} limits it to the named
interface. The command asks the operating system for configuration; it
doesn't send traffic to a destination. Look for \texttt{inet} for IPv4
and \texttt{inet6} for IPv6 if configured. A suffix like
\texttt{/8} or \texttt{/128} is an address prefix length, not a port
number. It describes how many leading bits identify a network prefix;
you don't need to calculate it here.

On macOS, FreeBSD, OpenBSD, or NetBSD, use the local \texttt{ifconfig}
form with only the interface name. Don't append setting words or addresses.

\begin{terminal}
ifconfig lo0
\end{terminal}

The field order varies. Some versions show an IPv4 netmask rather than
a slash prefix length; it conveys which address bits belong to the network
prefix. Look for an address, not an exact transcript match. An absent
IPv6 entry isn't by itself an error. If the named utility or interface
does not exist, use the system's network information panel rather than
installing a package or modifying an interface just for this lesson.

You can view the computer's configured name, if \texttt{hostname} is
available, by using it with no arguments or options:

\begin{terminal}
hostname
\end{terminal}

This is the local configuration's idea of a name; it doesn't prove that
another device can resolve it. Some systems have a short name, some have a
longer name with dots, and some have a name generated by the network or an
administrator. Output can reveal a person's name, a school or employer, or
the naming pattern of machines inside a workplace. For debugging your own
problem it may help to write ``confirmed I was on my laptop'' instead of
pasting the literal output into a public forum. There is no need here to
request every network adapter, DNS option, or address from your system.

\section{Connections produce different kinds of evidence}
\index{TCP}\index{TLS}
Once the client has an address, it tries to talk to the intended port.
TCP and UDP are distinct transport protocols, and both have port numbers
of their own. An HTTPS client may use TCP or, with HTTP/3, QUIC over UDP.
To reason about the page here, imagine the TCP case first. A refusal
normally means the connection was rejected promptly by an endpoint or
intermediary. The service may not be listening there, or policy may
actively reject the connection. A timeout means the program did not
receive what it needed before its deadline. It doesn't tell you which
device failed, filtered traffic, or simply responded too late. Clients
can time out during several stages, not only while opening the connection.
Figure~\ref{fig:37-layers} shows where those later stages fit.

Next comes the protected connection. TLS is the protocol that a conventional
HTTPS client uses to protect communication and check the service identity.
The client checks, among other things, whether the name it meant to contact
is covered by the certificate. A bad clock can make a legitimate certificate
appear outside its validity period; a wrong or untrusted certificate can
also signal a much more serious problem. Stop and ask the service owner
using a known channel rather than suppressing the check. Some public
networks have a sign-in page and can interrupt access until you follow the
network operator's official instructions. That does not make an unexpected
certificate safe to accept. Once TLS works, the web service still has to
process the request. A response like HTTP 404 (not found) isn't evidence
of absent Internet access; an HTTP responder answered for that request.
It may be an intermediary or deliberately conceal a resource.

There might also be a proxy between the application and the service.
A \FirstUse{proxy} is a program that makes or forwards requests on
someone else's behalf. For example, an employer may route work
browsers through a proxy that enforces access rules. It could be
the proxy that resolves the destination name, not the computer under
your hands. In that setting an application that ignores the proxy
isn't a clean comparison with the approved browser. If you need
support, say you used the ordinary work configuration. Don't bypass it
to make a troubleshooting result look simpler. Likewise, a VPN only
affects the traffic it is set up to handle. ``VPN appears connected''
and ``this request can reach the private service'' aren't equivalent
observations.

Be especially careful interpreting so-called connectivity tests for a
service. A response from a different protocol tells you that something
responded under the conditions of that test. It doesn't show a web
application can process bookings. Conversely, a service may correctly
ignore a test message altogether while responding to its normal
clients. Choose observations from the request that actually matters.
Using a book's example IP address, changing ports at random, or
requesting pages repeatedly teaches you little about the original
problem and can interfere with other people's work.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-37/layers.tikz}
\caption{In the TCP case, look for the last stage that actually worked.}\label{fig:37-layers}
\VisualDescription{Name lookup leads to transport, identity checking, and then an HTTP response. Any stage can fail while the preceding one succeeds.}
\end{figure}

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.45in}Y}
\toprule
Observation & Narrow next question \\
\midrule
Can't resolve name & Does the address match the one the owner supplied, and is this a name only available on an approved network? \\
Refused or timed out & Is this the intended service and port, and what stage did the actual client report? \\
Certificate warning & Are the name and local time correct, and what does the owner say through a known channel? \\
HTTP 401 or 403 & Does the request require a sign-in or permission? Follow the official access procedure. \\
HTTP 404 & Is the resource path still correct? Ask for the current link rather than guessing alternatives. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{dialoguebox}{The weather page}
\Brian{If the weather page loads, doesn't that settle the network question?}
\Stephen{It shows your computer can reach a site.}
\Brian{Or that my browser already had the weather page.}
\Stephen{Good catch. A fresh response is better evidence than a cached view.}
\Brian{Even a fresh forecast doesn't tell me whether the room-booking name works.}
\Stephen{Right. It rules out some broad failures, not a failure specific to the booking request.}
\Brian{So I should tell support which request gave which result, not say the Internet is down.}
\Stephen{And say whether you know it was a fresh request.}
\DialogueConclusion{Report the observation, not a diagnosis.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Practice on a fictional desk report}
Shell examples use \texttt{sh} syntax. The following practice uses ordinary
text files rather than sending even one request. Save these examples for
looking at a problem when you have enough context to describe it. Start
with a new directory; \cmd|mktemp -d| asks for a unique directory,
replacing the final \texttt{X}s in the trusted local temporary path
with a suffix. The utility is common but not required by POSIX.
The first line saves the resulting path in the shell variable
\texttt{desk}, whose quoted expansion is one operand. Each
\texttt{\&\&} runs the next command only when the previous
one succeeds. Confirm that \cmd|pwd| shows the new
\texttt{utp-desk} directory under \texttt{/tmp}; otherwise stop
without creating the log. Use a different trusted absolute
temporary-directory path if your system requires one.

\begin{terminal}
desk=$(mktemp -d /tmp/utp-desk.XXXXXXXX) &&
  cd "$desk" &&
  pwd
\end{terminal}

Make the file once, in this new directory. \cmd|printf| repeats the format
for each of the four arguments; shell continuation backslashes keep the
command on multiple input lines. The \cmd|>| writes and could truncate
\texttt{desk.log} if you repeated it somewhere else. The timestamps are
local practice clock labels, not test data from a live network.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' \
  '14:00 lookup: no answer recorded' \
  '14:05 lookup: IPv4 192.0.2.25' \
  '14:05 transport: connection opened' \
  '14:05 web: HTTP 404 for /rooms/tuesday' > desk.log
\end{terminal}

Read the whole report with \cmd|cat desk.log|, then use fixed-string search
to retrieve observations about one stage. \cmd|-F| keeps the search literal;
the pattern is the text before an ordinary clause on those lines, and the
last operand is the file to inspect.

\begin{terminal}
cat desk.log
grep -F 'lookup:' desk.log
grep -F 'web:' desk.log
\end{terminal}

Expect two lookup lines and one web line. If only the second lookup was
recorded, you couldn't demonstrate a change in lookup outcome. Likewise
the report's first line describes absent evidence, not necessarily a failed
lookup: it says ``no answer recorded,'' not ``NXDOMAIN.'' The latter is a
DNS protocol response that says the queried name does not exist in the
answering view. Whether or not your tool uses that term, distinguish a
negative answer from a failure to receive an answer. The two hypotheses
lead to different follow-up questions.

For the next example, look for a TLS observation, then immediately print
the status of that search. No matching line is expected. The single-quoted
format \cmd|'%s\n'| belongs to \cmd|printf|; \cmd|"$?"| is the previous
command's exit status. A \cmd|grep| status of 1 indicates no matching
lines; a status greater than 1 indicates an error. Don't infer that TLS was
skipped from a search with no matches. It only shows that those letters
followed by a colon are not present in the log.

\begin{terminal}
grep -F 'TLS:' desk.log
printf '%s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

In a real support exchange, share a short chronology with timezone, the
client type, a redacted resource description, exact non-sensitive error
words, and steps tried. Record when you switched networks, not the names
of all networks you can see. If calling the library, you could say:
``This afternoon my browser reached the booking site, but the room link
gave not found. I used the link from yesterday's email; where is the current
booking page?'' This is kinder to both parties than an attached dump of
every setting. For a service you don't administer, use its normal interface
and published support channels; don't try unrelated ports or devices.
There are no live connection exercises in this chapter.

If you make a second observation, change one relevant condition at a
time and record what changed. ``After checking the published URL, I saw
that Tuesday's link had been replaced'' is useful. ``I restarted,
changed networks, changed the address, and tried again'' doesn't tell
you which step mattered. In practice the next observation may be a
question for a person instead of a new command. A service owner can
say whether an old booking link expired or whether outsiders are
supposed to use a different portal. Nothing in the fictional desk log
would establish that. The terminal has given you a better question,
which is often the best possible result.

Keep the temporary directory until the exercises are done. To clean up,
use \cmd|pwd| first and check that you are in \cmd|"$desk"|. Remove only
\cmd|./desk.log| with \cmd|rm -i| (confirm only the practice file).
Use \cmd|cd && rmdir "$desk"| so removal is not attempted if
\cmd|cd| fails. \cmd|rmdir| refuses to remove a directory with
entries, so unexpected files are a reason to stop and look.

\begin{exercise}{Same host, another room}\label{ex:37-url}
Print a version of the example URL with path \texttt{/rooms/friday}.
Identify the pieces you didn't change and explain what changed without
opening the address. This creates no files; keep the desk log.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\cmd|printf '%s\n' 'https://booking.example.org:443/rooms/friday'|
prints the new URL. The scheme, host name, and port remain the same; only
the requested resource name changes. Nothing was fetched or modified.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{One interface, one claim}\label{ex:37-loopback}
Use the read-only loopback command for your system. Find an address if
configured, and say one thing the result establishes and one thing it
doesn't. If your tool is unavailable, use the printed addresses instead.
No cleanup is needed.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
In the Linux output, \texttt{inet 127.0.0.1/8}, if present, is evidence of
the local IPv4 loopback configuration. The comparable macOS/BSD line may
spell out the netmask. It says nothing about access to the library server.
A printed address illustrates notation only; it isn't evidence about
your machine.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Separate absence from error}\label{ex:37-status}
In the desk directory, search first for the fixed string
\texttt{transport:} in \texttt{desk.log}, then for
\texttt{TLS:} in the same file, then for \texttt{transport:}
in \texttt{absent.log}, which was not created. Check each status
before running another command. Finish by removing the desk log and
its directory as above.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\cmd|grep -F 'transport:' desk.log| prints one line and returns zero.
\cmd|grep -F 'TLS:' desk.log| prints nothing and returns one.
\cmd|grep -F 'transport:' absent.log| prints a diagnostic and returns a
status greater than 1; that is not a successful search with zero matches.
After checking \cmd|pwd|, \cmd|rm -i ./desk.log| removes the one practice
file on confirmation; \cmd|cd && rmdir "$desk"| finishes the cleanup.
\end{solution}

\begin{takeaways}
\item A name, an address, a port, and a resource path answer different questions.
\item Local observations describe local state; don't promote them into remote facts.
\item Record the last working stage and the first observed failure, with context and without secrets.
\end{takeaways}

